From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 02:38:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/25] runtime: add backend memory resilience

Expose process, allocator, cgroup, Node, cache, and control-plane memory ownership so operators can account for backend RSS and telemetry coverage. Report glibc arena count separately and preserve the distinction between logical allocator free space and resident memory.

Bound the local Node generation with an explicit V8 old-space share and graceful retirement based on sampled direct-child RSS, sustained cgroup pressure with material RSS, age, and lifetime imported-package count. Preserve generation fencing, active-request drain, and child reaping across replacement. Publish a shared cgroup-pressure signal for local Node and downstream owner-specific patches.

Validate configured worker, cache, Node, native, and kernel allowances against a finite cgroup limit at startup. At an earlier hysteretic cgroup boundary, run a cooldown-limited glibc trim, resample headroom, and add a separately configured lower external HTTP shedding boundary for new work while preserving internal dependency callbacks and observability endpoints.

Export bounded controller transitions, allocator-trim outcomes and before/after memory changes, arena availability, and Node pressure state and configuration. Document allocator-accounting limits, strict knob relationships, metric reset semantics, and the generic self-hosted operator contract without requiring context reuse.

A long-running multithreaded self-hosted backend under a 30 GiB cgroup limit reached 19.57 GiB process RSS and 20.85 GiB cgroup use while glibc reported 13.75 GiB of arena space, 10.08 GiB logically free, and 123 arenas. An explicit malloc_trim(0) reduced cgroup use by about 6.35 GiB in 4.122 seconds, while mmap allocations were negligible. This supports cgroup-aware accounting and controlled trimming and shows that default glibc retention was poorly matched to this workload. Logical free bytes are not resident bytes, and this observation is not a general allocator ranking.
